WB.L("contractor-or-employee", "life", "advanced", "Working on an ABN: contractor or employee?",
  "More Australians are picking up work as contractors. Here is how to tell if it is genuine, and what to sort out if you go it alone.",
  [
    ["Two ways of working", "Contracting can offer freedom, but it shifts tax, super, leave and risk from the business to you.",
      "Liam is offered a job building websites. The pay is good, but the business asks him to get an ABN and send invoices instead of going on the payroll. It sounds simple. Yet the difference between employee and independent contractor changes almost everything: who pays your tax and super, whether you get paid leave, whether an award covers you and what happens if the work dries up.\n\nPlenty of people contract happily, from tradies and consultants to designers, and enjoy the flexibility. But the label is not just paperwork. This lesson is general information, not legal, tax or financial advice, so check your own situation with official sources."],
    ["Employee or contractor?", "The real nature of the working relationship matters more than what the contract calls it.",
      "There is no single test. Courts and tribunals look at the whole relationship, including how it works in practice. Following 2024 changes to the Fair Work Act, the law makes clear that the real substance of the arrangement counts.\n\nSigns of employment include the business controlling how, when and where you work, being paid for your time rather than a result, using their equipment, working only for them, being part of their team and being unable to send someone else. Signs of genuine contracting include running your own business, choosing how to do the job, taking on financial risk, using your own tools, working for several clients and being able to delegate."],
    ["Sham contracting", "Calling an employee a contractor to avoid obligations is illegal.",
      "Sham contracting is when a business falsely presents employment as independent contracting. It can be deliberate, to avoid paying super, leave and award rates, or a genuine misunderstanding. It is against the law to misrepresent employment as contracting, to dismiss an employee in order to re engage them as a contractor doing much the same work, or to pressure someone into becoming a contractor with a false statement.\n\nIf you are really an employee, you may be entitled to back pay, super and leave even if you signed a contractor agreement and hold an ABN. If you are unsure, the Fair Work Ombudsman explains the difference, and the ATO offers an employee or contractor decision tool on its website."],
    ["ABN, tax and GST", "An ABN is free, but you must be running a business, and no one withholds tax for you.",
      "If you genuinely carry on a business, you can apply for an Australian Business Number at abr.gov.au. It is free, though many websites charge to do it for you. You will need your tax file number. Employees and hobbyists do not qualify. Quote it on invoices, as businesses may have to withhold tax from suppliers who do not.\n\nAs a contractor, the full invoice usually lands in your account with no tax taken out. That money is not all yours. Include it in your tax return, and the ATO may place you on pay as you go instalments. Many contractors move a set share of each payment into a separate tax account. You must register for GST once turnover reaches the threshold, or immediately in some industries, such as ride sourcing. Check current thresholds on the ATO website."],
    ["Safety net and pricing", "Contractors build their own safety net, so set rates that cover real costs and put agreements in writing.",
      "Genuine contractors often get no paid leave, employer super or workers compensation automatically. Many make voluntary super contributions, though some contractors paid mainly for their labour can still be entitled to super guarantee payments, so check ATO guidance. Consider public liability, professional indemnity and income protection insurance, and plan time off.\n\nA common mistake is setting a rate by dividing an employee salary by working hours. You must also cover super, unpaid leave, insurance, equipment, accounting fees and quiet periods, which is why contract rates often exceed the equivalent wage. Agree in writing on scope, price, payment terms, who owns the work and how either side can end the deal, and send clear invoices with your ABN. If a deal feels lopsided, get independent legal advice before signing."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What changes when you work as a contractor rather than an employee?", ["The business withholds your tax but pays no super","You are automatically covered by an award or agreement","You no longer have to pay income tax on your earnings","You handle your own tax and super; leave is not automatic"], 3, "Contracting shifts tax, super, leave and risk from the business to you."],
    [1, "What matters most in deciding whether someone is an employee?", ["What the signed contract calls the arrangement","Whether the worker has an ABN","The real substance of how the working relationship operates","Whether the worker is paid by invoice"], 2, "Courts look at the whole relationship, not just the label on the paperwork."],
    [2, "What is sham contracting?", ["Any contract between a business and a freelancer","Falsely presenting what is really employment as contracting","Contracting that pays less than an award","Working for several clients at once"], 1, "It is illegal, and a worker who is really an employee may be owed back pay, super and leave."],
    [3, "Which statement about an ABN is correct?", ["It is free to apply for, but you must be carrying on a business","It is available to every employee on request","It must be bought through a registration service","It removes the need to include income in your tax return"], 0, "You can apply for free at abr.gov.au, but only if you are genuinely running a business."],
    [4, "Why do contract rates usually need to be higher than an equivalent wage?", ["Clients are legally required to pay a contractor premium","Contractors always work longer hours than employees do","Tax is not charged on income earned from contracting","It must cover super, leave, insurance and quiet periods"], 3, "Dividing a salary by hours leaves out the costs an employer would normally carry."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"One line on your invoice can change your tax, super and rights. Which side are you on?"});

WB.L("leaving-a-job-well", "life", "beginner", "Leaving a job the right way",
  "How you leave a job can shape your references, your final pay and your reputation for years. Here is how to resign well.",
  [
    ["Endings are remembered", "How you leave can shape your references, final pay and reputation, because industries are smaller than they look.",
      "The manager you leave behind may one day interview you, become your client or be called as your referee. The colleague who covered your workload after a messy exit may turn up at your next workplace. A good exit protects your references, makes sure you receive everything you are owed and leaves the door open.\n\nA bad exit, such as walking out mid shift, posting complaints online or vanishing without a handover, can follow you for years. Leaving well does not mean pretending everything was perfect. It means being clear, professional and fair, even if you are glad to be going. This is general information, not legal advice. The Fair Work Ombudsman has details for your situation."],
    ["Notice and the conversation", "Check your required notice, tell your manager in person first, then confirm in writing.",
      "Your notice period is usually set by your award, enterprise agreement or contract, so check all three. One to four weeks is common, often depending on how long you have worked there. Casual employees often have different rules. If you do not give the required notice, some awards and agreements allow your employer to deduct an amount from your final pay.\n\nYour manager should hear the news from you, not the grapevine. Ask for a short private meeting, say you have decided to leave, give your last day and thank them. You need not explain where you are going or air every grievance. An exit interview suits that better. Then send a short resignation letter or email with the date, your role, a clear statement that you are resigning, your last day and a thank you. Keep a copy."],
    ["Counter offers", "Think carefully before accepting a counter offer, and stay calm if the reaction is tough.",
      "An employer may respond to your resignation with more money, a new title or a promise that things will change. It can be flattering, and occasionally it is exactly what you wanted. But ask why you started looking in the first place. If the reasons were pay alone, a counter offer might solve it. If they were culture, management, workload or growth, money rarely fixes those for long. Consider, too, why it took a resignation to get the offer.\n\nSome managers react badly, with anger or guilt. Stay polite, firm and brief. The decision is yours. You must not be threatened or punished for exercising workplace rights, and the Fair Work Ombudsman can give information if things go wrong."],
    ["Hand over properly", "A clear handover is the best way to protect your reputation on the way out.",
      "Use your notice period to make life easy for whoever takes over. Write a handover document covering current projects, where key files and passwords are stored through approved channels, upcoming deadlines, regular tasks and important contacts. Note what is half finished and what happens next. Offer to train a colleague or your replacement. Be honest about what you cannot finish.\n\nKeep working properly until your last day, because coasting is noticed. Return laptops, phones, keys and uniforms. Do not take confidential information, client lists or company files, which could breach your contract or the law. Say goodbye to colleagues and swap personal contact details with those you want to stay in touch with."],
    ["Final pay and goodbyes", "Make sure your final pay includes everything you are owed, then leave on good terms.",
      "Final pay should include wages to your last day and other outstanding amounts. Under the National Employment Standards, unused annual leave is paid out, including leave loading if your award, agreement or contract provides for it. Unused personal or carer's leave generally is not. Long service leave depends mostly on your state or territory and sometimes your award. Check your final payslip against your leave balance, confirm your final super is paid, and download your payslips before system access ends. Raise anything missing in writing, then contact the Fair Work Ombudsman if needed.\n\nAsk a manager or senior colleague to be a referee. Give constructive exit feedback, update LinkedIn only after telling your manager, send a farewell message and avoid criticising your old employer publicly. Many people return later."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why does the way you leave a job matter?", ["It changes how much notice you are required to give","Past managers may become referees, clients or colleagues","Your old employer must publish a reference about you","It affects only the amount on your final payslip"], 1, "Industries are small, so a professional exit protects your reputation and references."],
    [1, "Where is your required notice period usually set?", ["In your award, agreement or employment contract","By your manager when you hand in your resignation","By the Fair Work Ombudsman for each employee","By a single national rule of exactly two weeks"], 0, "Check all three documents, because some let employers deduct pay if you give short notice."],
    [2, "You receive a counter offer after resigning. What is a wise first step?", ["Accept at once, because the offer proves you are valued","Refuse without thinking, because counter offers never work","Accept it, then keep job hunting in secret","Ask why you wanted to leave and whether money would fix it"], 3, "If your reasons were culture, workload or growth, extra money rarely solves them."],
    [3, "What should a good handover include?", ["Copies of client lists to keep for your own records","A list of your colleagues' faults for the replacement","Current projects, deadlines, regular tasks and key contacts","Only a quick verbal summary on your very last day"], 2, "A written handover makes life easy for your replacement and protects your reputation."],
    [4, "Which is generally included in your final pay?", ["Unused personal or carer's leave, but no annual leave","Unused annual leave, but not usually unused personal leave","Neither type of leave, which lapses when you resign","All leave types, including sick leave"], 1, "Annual leave is paid out under the National Employment Standards; personal leave generally is not."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"The last two weeks of a job can matter more than the last two years. Here is why."});

